    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-8">Our systemic approach</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">Family therapy is based on the idea that problems don't exist in isolation — they arise and are maintained within the context of relationships. Rather than focusing on "the problem person," we look at how the family as a whole functions and communicates. This systemic perspective opens up new possibilities for change that individual therapy alone might not achieve.</p>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Who attends?</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Family therapy can involve any combination of family members — the whole family, parents only, siblings, or parent and child. We'll discuss what makes most sense for your situation.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">How long does it take?</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Family therapy tends to be shorter-term than individual therapy, often 6–12 sessions. Some families return for periodic sessions as needs arise.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
